#X msg 130 190 0;
#X text 30 20 404 ACCELERANDO -- a CONTINUOUS sweep from slow to the ceiling and back. Send go to start and stop to end., f 100;
#X text 430 250 THE INTERVAL ADVANCES ONCE PER TICK \, and that is the whole point. Driving it from outside failed: metro only applies a new interval on its NEXT tick \, so at 4 per second nine of every ten updates from a 25 ms sender were discarded -- the slow end crawled in coarse steps while the fast end was smooth \, which is heard as a jump rather than a sweep. One change per tick is smooth BY CONSTRUCTION and is what an accelerando actually is., f 52;
#X text 430 620 x0.95 EACH TICK is geometric \, which is right because pitch is logarithmic -- a linear ramp would spend nearly all its time at the slow end. moses 2 and moses 400 are the turning points: below 2 ms it starts slowing \, above 400 ms it starts speeding up \, so it runs back and forth until stopped., f 52;
#X text 30 660 ⚠️ 2 ms is the floor ON PURPOSE. That is 500 per second -- the measured useful ceiling. Pd's scheduler tick is 64 samples so past about 689 per second several events fall due in one block and go out together \, which is ragged rather than faster., f 100;
